  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Hemmed in between limestone cliffs over 400 metres high, the Clue d'Aiglun is one of the most spectacular sites on the Estéron river, with its turquoise pools and potholes carved into the rock.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Randoxygène (Alpes-Maritimes department) presents the Clue d'Aiglun as "perhaps the most famous canyon in the department": it is above all a canyoning site, about 1 km long, rated 4/5 for flood risk. The walk starts from the Sallagriffon entrance car park (750 m): allow about 45 minutes via the Mont Saint-Martin path to the start of the clue, and around twenty minutes for the return on foot along the Estéron bank. You can also reach the start of the clue from Aiglun via the Mont Saint-Martin path (about 1h45). None of the official sources consulted gives a rule on dogs; the Préalpes d'Azur Regional Nature Park advises, in the valley's grazing areas, preferably not bringing your dog and, otherwise, keeping it firmly on a leash. For the descent, Randoxygène describes long narrow pools where you have to swim, abseils and sections on ledges fitted with handrails where you move self-belayed on lanyards, only at low flow (permitted from 1 April to 31 October, every day): it is a technical activity for equipped canyoners, not an outing to consider with a dog. With your dog, only the approach on foot to the start of the clue is conceivable (climbing path then descending path, with ridge sections), on a leash and without entering the Estéron riverbed.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: stick to the paths and don't venture into the Estéron riverbed with your dog — the flow can rise fast after a storm upstream.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Clue de Saint-Auban, in the same Estéron valley, offers another swimming pool with its own mill ruins (see the dedicated listing).</p>
    </div>

